Title: KEYS= explicit per-key selection and rename (provider:SRC=DST)
Created: 2026-07-14
Author: Minh Ha-Duong

--- log ---
2026-07-14T00:00Z Minh Ha-Duong created

--- body ---
## Context

`scripts/bash-env.sh` loads provider secrets least-privilege via a
`KEYS=prov1,prov2` line in the untrusted project `$PWD/.env`: for each
validated provider it sources the whole user-owned
`$HOME/.config/keys/<prov>.env`. This over-shares when a provider file holds
several keysets. `openrouter.env`, for example, defines
`OPENROUTER_API_KEY_AEDIST`, `OPENROUTER_API_KEY_KIEU`, and `EXPIRED_*`;
sourcing it whole drops every one of those into the environment, and none of
the suffixed names matches the plain `OPENROUTER_API_KEY` a consumer expects.

Least-privilege must apply at the **export boundary**, not the filesystem —
the author's decision is to keep one file per provider (no file proliferation,
no per-consumer key files) and instead select the exact variable a project
needs, optionally renaming it, at load time.

## Grammar

Each comma-separated entry in the `KEYS=` value takes one of three forms:

- `provider` — source the whole `$HOME/.config/keys/<provider>.env` (unchanged
  behaviour; still supported).
- `provider:VAR` — from `<provider>.env`, export ONLY `VAR`, under the name `VAR`.
- `provider:SRC=DST` — from `<provider>.env`, export ONLY `SRC`, renamed to
  `DST`. No other variable from that file enters the environment.

No naming convention: selection and rename are explicit and verbose, with no
suffix-stripping magic.

Validation: `provider` matches `^[a-z0-9-]+$` (as today); `SRC`/`DST` each match
`^[A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_]*$`. An entry failing validation is skipped with a stderr
warning `bash-env: ignoring invalid KEYS entry: <entry>` (default-deny
preserved). A named `SRC` absent from the file warns
`bash-env: KEYS var not found: <provider>:<SRC>` and is skipped. A missing
provider file warns and is skipped, as today.

## Mechanism (security-critical)

A `provider:...` entry must NOT source the provider file into the live
environment. Instead the file is sourced in an ISOLATED subshell that extracts
ONLY the requested `SRC` value; the parent exports `DST` with that value
assigned LITERALLY (command substitution captures the string — it is never
`eval`'d), so a value containing spaces or `$(...)` is preserved verbatim, never
executed. Only `DST` lands in the real environment; `SRC`'s siblings (KIEU,
EXPIRED_*) die with the subshell. Bare `provider` entries keep the existing
whole-file source path (no subshell). The script runs on every bash subprocess,
so a subshell is spawned only for `provider:` selection entries.

## Exit criteria

- [ ] The three entry forms parse and resolve correctly (bare, `:VAR`, `:SRC=DST`).
- [ ] For a selection entry, ONLY `DST` is exported — not `SRC`, not the file's
      other variables.
- [ ] Validation rejects bad providers/SRC/DST and malformed entries with the
      documented warnings; the shell survives (default-deny).
- [ ] A named-but-absent `SRC` warns and is skipped.
- [ ] Value integrity: a value with a space and a `$(...)` literal is exported
      verbatim, not executed.
- [ ] New `tests/test_bash_env_keys_selection_explicit.sh` covers all of the
      above; the existing bash-env suites still pass (no regression).
- [ ] Header comment documents the three forms and that selection happens at the
      export boundary.
- [ ] `make lint` and the bash-env shell suites pass; `./tickets/erg check
      tickets/` passes.
